# =============================================================================
# run_opera.config - OPERA-RADAR-driven training runbook
# =============================================================================
#
# What this file is
# -----------------
# A documentation-only, comment-only walkthrough of the full pipeline when
# you want sample selection driven by OPERA rainfall-rate activity. Every
# command here is meant to be copy-pasted (without the leading `# `) from
# the project root.
#
# Track recap: the "OPERA track" gates the training sample list by
# DBSCAN clusters in the OPERA rainfall_rate field via identify_patches
# --source opera. Sample selection is decoupled from lightning - any
# timestep where OPERA saw convective rainfall is a candidate, whether
# or not LINET fired. Use this when you want a broader, larger dataset
# (typically several times the lightning-track count) or when training
# for OPERA rainfall regression / classification. For lightning-driven
# sample selection, see `run_lightning.config` instead.
#
# Notation in the parallelism notes:
#   [single]  single-threaded, CPU-bound
#   [mp:N]    Python multiprocessing pool of up to N workers
#   [GPU]     CUDA / cuDNN / mixed-precision tensor cores
#   [I/O]     network / disk bound (SFTP, large file transfers)
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 0 - Configure cadence  [single, ~1 second]
# =============================================================================
#
# Writes our_data/timestep_config.json. Single source of truth for every
# downstream script. Re-run ONLY when you edit product_cadences.config.
#
#     python validate_timestep.py --step_minutes 15
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 1 - Acquire raw inputs (per product)  [I/O]
# =============================================================================
#
# OPERA-track inputs: MTG, OPERA. Each pipeline filters
# in `pipeline_<product>.py` BEFORE transfer based on
# `products.<product>.filter` so only cadence-aligned files cross the wire.
#
# Lightning is OPTIONAL on the OPERA track - skip it if you don't want
# lightning channels in the input stack. The mode you pick in step 11
# decides whether the model consumes lightning inputs.
#
# MTG FCI:
#     python our_data/satellite_data/pipeline_msg_mtg.py \
#         -s 2025/04/01-0000 -e 2025/09/30-2350 \
#         --password_file password.txt \
#         --products_file our_data/satellite_data/satellite_products.json
#
# OPERA radar (THE DRIVER for this track):
#     python our_data/opera_data/pipeline_opera.py \
#         -s 2025/04/01-0000 -e 2025/09/30-2350 \
#         --password_file password.txt
#
# Lightning KMLs (optional - only if your chosen mode uses lightning
# channels as inputs):
#     python our_data/raw_data/lightning_arrange.py \
#         -s our_data/raw_data/lightning \
#         --start-date 2025-04-01 --end-date 2025-09-30
#     python our_data/lightning_data/read_kml_version2.py
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 2 - Reproject satellite + OPERA to Romania grid  [mp:6 (--workers)]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reprojects each product through a precomputed KD-tree mapping. Per-
# category logs land at our_data/reprojected_data/reproject_<cat>.log
# and are aggregated into our_data/reprojected_data/errors.txt after
# every run.
#
#     python reproject.py --all --workers 12
#
# Or per-category if you only need one:
#     python reproject.py --satellite MTG --workers 12
#     python reproject.py --opera --workers 12
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 3 - Per-product coverage summaries  [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Both emit <product>_summary.csv + <product>_missing_timesteps.json
# at project root. Include the lightning summarizer only if you arranged
# + processed lightning in step 1.
#
#     python our_data/satellite_data/summarize_mtg.py
#     python our_data/opera_data/summarize_opera_data.py
#     python our_data/lightning_data/summarize_lightning_data.py   # optional
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 4 - Per-patch activity index (OPERA-DRIVEN)  [single, DBSCAN]
# =============================================================================
#
# identify_patches.py runs DBSCAN (threshold=10, eps=5, min_samples=20)
# on the reprojected OPERA rainfall_rate field and writes
# our_data/patch_index/patch_index.csv keyed by master HHMM. Note: this
# is the SAME file extract_patch_seq reads under --source dbscan -
# the downstream "dbscan source" means "the patch index produced by
# identify_patches.py" regardless of which sensor drove the clustering.
#
#     python identify_patches.py --source opera
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 5 - Cross-product manifest  [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Intersects MTG and OPERA presence per timestep. Lightning is
# typically NOT used as a gate on the OPERA track (that would constrain
# OPERA samples to lightning-active windows - exactly the opposite of
# what this track is for). Add it back with --summary lightning=... +
# --active lightning=... only if you specifically want the intersection
# of both.
#
#     python intersect_product_coverage.py \
#         --summary  mtg=mtg_summary.csv \
#         --summary  opera=opera_summary.csv \
#         --errors_log our_data/reprojected_data/errors.txt
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 6 - Continuous sequences + Czibula split (DBSCAN source)
#          [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads patch_index.csv (the OPERA-DBSCAN output from step 4),
# intersects with the manifest (final cross-product gate), and writes
# per-source CSVs:
#     our_data/train_data_dbscan.csv
#     our_data/validation_data_dbscan.csv
#     our_data/test_data_dbscan.csv
#     our_data/sequence_meta_dbscan.json
# Plus an audit CSV listing every (date, time) the manifest dropped:
#     our_data/extract_patch_seq_drops_dbscan.csv
#
#     python extract_patch_seq_for_datasets.py --source dbscan
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 7 - Extract patch arrays from reprojected data  [mp]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads patch_index.csv AND timestep_manifest.csv, then slices 256x256
# patches from each reprojected product. Resumable via the manifest gate.
#
#     python extract_patches.py
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 8 - Per-variable normalization stats  [single, optional GPU via CuPy]
# =============================================================================
#
# Mean/std per variable computed on the TRAIN split only (snapping to
# per-product filters first). The CuPy GPU path kicks in automatically
# when cupy is importable.
#
#     python compute_normalization_stats.py
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 9 - Build TFRecord datasets (DBSCAN source)  [single, I/O-bound]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads sequence_meta_dbscan.json + the per-source train/val/test CSVs.
# Writes sharded TFRecord files to our_data/datasets/<mode>_dbscan/
# along with a per-shard metadata.json that train_models reconstructs
# the parse signature from.
#
# Canonical OPERA-track modes target the OPERA rainfall_rate 5-class
# label:
#
#     python create_datasets.py --mode mtg_opera_radar_only  --source dbscan
#     python create_datasets.py --mode mtg_opera_mtgmr       --source dbscan
#
# Or for an OPERA-driven run with lightning channels in the input stack:
#     python create_datasets.py --mode mtg_radar             --source dbscan
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 10 - TRAIN MODEL  [GPU, mixed precision fp16]
# =============================================================================
#
# ### CUT-OFF: two paths from here ###############################
#
# Path A (SIMPLE, recommended for a first run): train the standard
# coalition model and stop. One single-output forecaster, no transformer
# head, same architecture train_models has shipped with all along.
# Mention this is the most direct way to validate the OPERA-track
# dataset before reaching for the transformer head.
#
#     python train_models.py \
#         --config training.config \
#         --mode mtg_opera_mtgmr \
#         --source dbscan \
#         --stage base
#
# Path B (DOMAIN ADAPTATION with Swin transformer head): run the base
# stage as above, then in the SAME process load it, freeze the backbone,
# graft a 2-block Swin head (8x8 windowed attention + 3 per-lead-time
# projection heads) on top, and fine-tune that head with AdamW. All
# hyperparameters come from the [finetune] section of training.config.
#
#     python train_models.py \
#         --config training.config \
#         --mode mtg_opera_mtgmr \
#         --source dbscan \
#         --stage both
#
# Run every mode listed in [modes].run back-to-back by omitting --mode.
# --source and --stage still apply to every mode in the run list:
#     python train_models.py \
#         --config training.config \
#         --source dbscan \
#         --stage both
#
# All artefacts land under ./models/ with `_<mode>_dbscan` suffixes:
#     coalition_mtg_opera_mtgmr_dbscan.keras            (base)
#     coalition_mtg_opera_mtgmr_dbscan_finetuned.keras  (Swin head)
#     history_mtg_opera_mtgmr_dbscan.json
#     history_mtg_opera_mtgmr_dbscan_finetuned.json
#     checkpoints/*_latest.keras                      (resumable per-epoch)
#
# Resume support: each stage writes its own per-epoch checkpoint so a
# crash mid-run only loses the partial epoch. Pass --fresh to force a
# clean start.
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 11 - Evaluate  [GPU]
# =============================================================================
#
# For the BASE model:
#     python evaluate_coalition.py
#     python bundle_eval_scores.py
#
# For the FINE-TUNED model: point evaluate_coalition.py at
# coalition_<run_tag>_finetuned.keras instead of the base checkpoint
# (the metadata.json schema is the same so no other change is needed).
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Notes
# =============================================================================
#
# - The OPERA-track dataset is typically several times larger than the
#   lightning-track one because OPERA fires on any convective rainfall,
#   not just lightning-producing storms. The Swin head's defaults
#   (head_dropout = 0.1, c_shared = 64) are sized with this in mind -
#   you can usually run base training with more epochs and a longer
#   warmup here than on the lightning track without overfitting.
#
# - The `mtg_opera_*` modes share the same target (OPERA rainfall
#   5-class) but differ in the input subsets they consume. The Swin
#   head can be added to any subset; the fine-tune flow doesn't change.
#
# - When the second machine runs the lightning track in parallel (see
#   `run_lightning.config`), the suffixed checkpoint / model paths
#   guarantee the two runs don't clobber each other if you ever rsync
#   the outputs into a single directory.
#
# - If you ever change --step_minutes in step 0, you must re-run
#   reproject.py (the .npy files are at the OLD cadence after snapping)
#   and every step after it. The on-disk filter-aligned filenames are
#   incompatible across step-minute changes.
